    "q-350210": {
      "dom_id": "step-q350210",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{3{ln}^{2}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | ${e}^{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{3{ln}^{2}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}{ln}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{3}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & ={u}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =({3}^{3}-{0}^{3}) \\\\  & =27-0 \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
